WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.432
The Zhou dynasty had just been founded, and the young King Cheng was still a child.

00:00:03.782 --> 00:00:06.734
His uncle, the Duke of Zhou, became his most important assisting minister.

00:00:07.084 --> 00:00:10.636
The world had only just been settled, and people's hearts were still unsettled.

00:00:10.986 --> 00:00:14.298
The Duke of Zhou handled state affairs while also teaching the young king.

00:00:14.648 --> 00:00:18.176
He told Cheng that a king must first learn to think of the people.

00:00:18.526 --> 00:00:21.214
Though young, King Cheng trusted the Duke of Zhou deeply.

00:00:21.564 --> 00:00:24.924
Traditional accounts say the Duke of Zhou put the rites and music in order.

00:00:25.274 --> 00:00:27.794
Later generations called this 'establishing the rites and creating the music.'

00:00:28.144 --> 00:00:31.456
Ministers and common people alike watched this grand ceremony.

00:00:31.806 --> 00:00:34.758
Rites were not only ceremonies — they also taught people how to treat one another.

00:00:35.108 --> 00:00:38.348
People of different stations each had rules they were expected to follow.

00:00:38.698 --> 00:00:42.178
Elders and juniors respected one another, and families grew more harmonious.

00:00:42.528 --> 00:00:45.288
When King Cheng grew up, the Duke of Zhou returned power to him.

00:00:45.638 --> 00:00:48.590
The Duke stepped back to a minister's place and continued to assist the king.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.388
Everyone admired the Duke for not clinging to power.

00:00:51.738 --> 00:00:55.290
The Duke of Zhou's ideas influenced many later generations.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.480
He became a symbol of the wise minister and of loyalty in Chinese history.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.590
These institutions became the foundation for later dynasties.

